e{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334023 13194139535150 true] [17592186334022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334024 13194139535150 true] [17592186334022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334025 13194139535150 true] [17592186334022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334026 13194139535150 true] [17592186334022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334027 13194139535150 true] [17592186334022 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535150 true] [17592186334022 1041 "selection" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334022 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334023 13194139535150 true] [17592186334022 1110 #uuid "dea87252-16b5-5057-9ca7-d8e94d8d267d" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334023 1034 "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535150 true] [17592186334023 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535150 true] [17592186334023 1111 #uuid "ec658ba7-e967-53d2-b7a5-46ef3845b9a3" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334024 1034 "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535150 true] [17592186334024 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535150 true] [17592186334024 1111 #uuid "24fecbcd-671e-5be7-8f3d-4d81ab09bddd" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334025 1034 "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535150 true] [17592186334025 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535150 true] [17592186334025 1111 #uuid "c9b29140-5283-5e62-b4ee-c41f0020750a" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334026 1034 "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535150 true] [17592186334026 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535150 true] [17592186334026 1111 #uuid "e0a657e5-a5af-57ad-af07-4c7ea7556e57" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334027 1034 "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535150 true] [17592186334027 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535150 true] [17592186334027 1111 #uuid "7602d930-bd07-50d5-839b-8dafc8b23da3" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334028 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334029 13194139535150 true] [17592186334028 1089 "q-85182" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334028 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535150 true] [17592186334028 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/51439321ae9d3760eb48d54351dbd519efe290c77d9197ca55aa590ce79e91a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334028 1113 #uuid "fd504b15-bc52-5a08-812b-fde09422adac" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334028 1145 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =0,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/bf2d20acbb93d3c61e9890a49d1c15a08c2b4e18b474da421bded7cf3dafc297.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334030 13194139535150 true] [17592186334029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334031 13194139535150 true] [17592186334029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334032 13194139535150 true] [17592186334029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334033 13194139535150 true] [17592186334029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334034 13194139535150 true] [17592186334029 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535150 true] [17592186334029 1041 "selection" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334029 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334034 13194139535150 true] [17592186334029 1110 #uuid "5b9741a1-096b-5dea-8215-2114e0d558e0" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334030 1034 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535150 true] [17592186334030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535150 true] [17592186334030 1111 #uuid "e09fa82b-f6ce-55f8-8aaa-4ed16725fb62" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334031 1034 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535150 true] [17592186334031 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535150 true] [17592186334031 1111 #uuid "e7ae53ac-ece4-5e32-9274-27693c9942c7" 13194139535150 true] [17592186334032 1034 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\fr